**user**:
> I usually think of BCRs as the NPV of benefits divided by the NPV of costs, over multiple years. Is there a term for BCRs that only consider the benefits and costs incurred in a particular year?
**assistant**:
> There isn’t a widely standardized special term that replaces “BCR” in that case; people usually just qualify it. Common phrasings are:
> 
> - **Annual benefit–cost ratio**  
> - **Year‑t benefit–cost ratio** or **BCR in year t**  
> - **Undiscounted annual BCR** (if you want to be explicit that you’re not discounting)
> 
> In more formal writing, you might see something like:
> 
> > \( \text{BCR}_t = \dfrac{B_t}{C_t} \)
> 
> and then referred to as “the period‑t benefit–cost ratio” or “the benefit–cost ratio in year t,” contrasted with:
> 
> > \( \text{BCR} = \dfrac{\sum_t \frac{B_t}{(1+r)^t}}{\sum_t \frac{C_t}{(1+r)^t}} \)
> 
> which is the usual NPV-based BCR.
> 
> If you want a crisp term in your own work, I’d suggest:
> 
> - “**Static (year‑t) BCR**” vs. “**dynamic (NPV) BCR**”
> 
> and define them once at the start. That’s clear and hard to misinterpret.
